WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.969
-Look at that.
-We have, like, real people.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.137
-Real people.
-In the same place.

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:05.604
-Standing ovation for you.
Yeah, of course.

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:07.540
-Feels like yesteryear.
-Yeah.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.409
Thank you so much for --
for being here, in person.

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:12.345
-No, thank you for having me.
-Well, you have a little

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.947
time off before you're just
taking over the world.

00:00:14.947 --> 00:00:17.383
September's a big month for you.
-It is a big month, yeah.

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:19.352
-I saw, though --
I follow you on Instagram.

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:20.553
You're a good follow.
-Okay.

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.321
-And I saw that you --
-Oh.

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:24.223
-You went golfing.
-Do you love Topgolf?

00:00:24.223 --> 00:00:26.592
-I've never been to a Topgolf.
-What?!

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:27.760
-I've never been.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.162
I didn't know
that you were a golfer.

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:31.731
-No. That's different.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:34.500
That's like saying someone that
likes to putt-putt is a golfer.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.668
No, no, no.
-Oh.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:36.736
-Which I'm not good
at putt-putt, either.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:38.605
But -- no, no.
I mean, I can like --

00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:40.473
I'm like Happy Gilmore,
like, when I golf.

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:42.308
Like, I can hit it really far.
-Uh-huh.

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.811
-But I'm not good at putting.
So -- And I'm really good

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:46.679
at driving the cart.
Like, really good.

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:48.314
With a drink.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:51.250
But I don't --
No, Topgolf is like a game.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.585
Well, it's a bar.
It's like an atmosphere.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:53.953
It's a vibe.
It's like a club,

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:55.254
but you're, like,
playing this game.

00:00:55.254 --> 00:00:56.689
You're just trying
to nail the balls, like,

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:59.225
into these certain places,
and you play games with people.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.693
And I like playing
'cause I always win.

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:03.196
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, exactly, right.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:06.031
-Yeah, I could hit it
really far, so...

00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:09.101
-When you're out, do you ever --
do you ever hear your music,

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:12.305
like, over the loud speakers
or anything?

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:15.508
-Not -- I mean, maybe like a --
like a remix or something.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.677
But, no, I feel like my --
this is a real thing.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.546
I feel like my music is, like,
so family-safe --

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:22.948
Like, you hear me in
dentist offices or like --

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:24.784
[ Laughter ]
Like, I feel like every time

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.320
my family or friends go to,
like, a dentist or a doctor,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.888
like, they're always like,
"Oh, I heard your song

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.223
again, today."
I'm like, "Sure you did."

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:32.458
Like, it's like -- it's like --
I am this --

00:01:32.458 --> 00:01:35.295
I am on the station that is
played in, like, grocery stores

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.130
and dentist offices.
-Yeah, you are.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.598
-I'm telling you.
Yeah.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.867
-What do you do if you're in a
grocery store and you come on?

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.835
-Well, sing along and dance.
Yes.

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:44.770
I'm a total tool.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.507
-You would do that?
-I literally -- one time in --

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:49.341
I actually --
Actually, I don't even know

00:01:49.341 --> 00:01:50.643
if there was music playing
this one time.

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:52.178
It was recently,
'cause I like going to Lowe's.

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:53.646
I'm like --
I love gardening and stuff.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:56.316
So, I was in Lowe's, and I was
just having a good day,

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:59.318
and I feel like you should
really lean into that right now

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:01.287
in this point in life
if you're having a good day.

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.989
And so, I leaned in,
and I just started dancing

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:06.859
and embarrassing my friend
Trisha in Lowe's,

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:08.027
and just started dancing
and singing.

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:09.996
-I did a similar thing
in Target.

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:11.764
-Were you on your knees?
I did a full on --

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:14.267
Got on my knees --
I was very into it.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.469
-Oh, no, I had the --
I was doing, like, a line dance

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.537
with my -- with my cart.
-Sure.

00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:20.372
-So I was, like,
getting into it.

00:02:20.372 --> 00:02:21.508
-Oh, yeah.
-It was -- It was

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:24.310
"Too Hot to Trot,"
by The Commodores.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.646
-Sure.
-♪ Too hot -- ♪

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:27.647
Yeah, that's it.

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:28.915
♪♪

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:30.216
-Yeah, so this jam's on,
it's like...

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:31.517
-Yes.
[ Cheers ]

00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:33.052
-And you're just working it.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.454
-♪ Too hot to trot, now, baby ♪

00:02:35.454 --> 00:02:37.123
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:37.123 --> 00:02:40.126
♪ Too hot to stop ♪
Whoo!

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:41.627
I was into it!
I go --

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:43.429
First of all, I love Target,
by the way.

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:45.598
-I love Target.
-I freaked out!

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:48.033
I go, "What?!"
I had no idea.

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:50.003
-Get it, playlist. Yeah.
-30 stores --

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.038
30 stores in the store.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:54.674
I'm like,
there's a produce section,

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:55.841
there's a lawyer.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:56.942
-You can get bananas
and underwear.

00:02:56.942 --> 00:02:58.611
-They have everything
you could --

00:02:58.611 --> 00:02:59.946
I've never been
to a store like that.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:02.281
And then great music playlist?
-Yes.

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:04.049
Well, that's the same thing.
They were playing stuff.

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:06.185
I was dancing in Lowe's, so...
-I love that you do that.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.588
-Yeah.
-Do you know that

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:12.058
"Since You've Been Gone"
is Tony Hawk's hype song?

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:14.026
-No. What?
Is that a joke?

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:15.395
-No. It's for real.
-Oh.

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:17.630
-For real for real.
Tony Hawk, the famous,

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:22.001
legendary skateboarder, to get
hyped up for anything competing,

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:23.235
he'll play
"Since You've Been Gone."

00:03:23.235 --> 00:03:25.905
That's his hype song.
-This is my thing.

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:28.541
I feel like I always hear people
talking about how, like,

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:31.711
my music is always on like a --
some Peloton playlist

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:33.612
or some workout like,
"Oh, it's my workout mix."

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:35.114
Like, everyone's --
And I'm like,

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:36.849
"It does not make me
want to work out."

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:38.885
[ Laughter ]
So, um, what?

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.720
Why doesn't it
have the same effect?

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.856
It's like...
Like, doesn't make me

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.892
want to run, but great for you.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:48.360
-Do you have a song that you
put on if you're working out?

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.096
-I have a play--
Well, okay.

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:52.431
Dwayne Johnson has a really good
playlist on Spotify.

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:54.433
Like, if you're looking
for workout playlists.

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:56.603
-See, I think it's intimidating.
I can't do that.

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:57.836
-No, okay.
-I'm never gonna look like

00:03:57.836 --> 00:04:01.708
Dwayne -- Dwayne "The Rock,"
or Dwayne Johnson?

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.276
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:04.710
-There might be
a Dwayne Johnson out there

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:05.978
that I might look like,
but I'm not gonna

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:07.179
look like The Rock.

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:08.581
-I was on the treadmill for,
like, my heart,

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:10.549
not, like, to lose weight.
I wasn't, like, going for it.

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:13.652
-Yeah, you just want to live.
-Just to try to live my life.

00:04:13.652 --> 00:04:16.022
So, no, but I was on it,
and he has --

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:17.756
There's this first one
on one of his playlists,

00:04:17.756 --> 00:04:22.094
and it's called "Coming Undone,"
and it's Eric Zayne, maybe.

00:04:22.094 --> 00:04:23.962
I had never heard
of this artist, but I love it,

00:04:23.962 --> 00:04:25.864
'cause I have to listen
to something that's like,

00:04:25.864 --> 00:04:27.433
"I'm a badass."

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:28.968
Like, if I'm on a treadmill,
I'm like, "Yes."

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.503
Like, I don't understand
how people listen to, like,

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:32.705
calming stuff.
-On TV, no.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.740
I do television.
-I can't do that, either,

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.776
'cause then I just want
to sit down with, like, food.

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.544
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.946
You're just like, "What?"
Or like a podcast?

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:43.216
I'm like, "This is not making me
want to keep doing this."

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:45.184
-Yeah.
By the way, your show --

00:04:45.184 --> 00:04:46.552
By the way, congrats.
-Thank you.

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:48.921
-You won another three Emmys
since I saw you last time.

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.122
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I know!

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:51.724
-Congratulations!
-I know.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.259
-You're doing such a great job.
-Thank you.

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:54.493
-It's a great show.
-Thank you.

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:56.729
-People love it.
-Who would have thought?

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.599
-You're just honest and funny,
and it's positive.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:01.034
I love it.
-Remember when I was here

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:02.769
a long time ago, though, and
I was like, "What am I doing?"

00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:04.136
and then you were, like,
giving me tips.

00:05:04.136 --> 00:05:05.437
It was right before it came on,
I was like,

00:05:05.437 --> 00:05:06.606
"I don't know
what I'm gonna do."

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:08.707
-Yeah.
Oh, yeah, I said,

00:05:08.707 --> 00:05:10.175
"Dude, you got to get, like,
some cooking segments."

00:05:10.175 --> 00:05:11.443
-You were like,
"You got to cook."

00:05:11.443 --> 00:05:12.745
And I go, "I don't cook."
-"I hate cooking."

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.013
-What the hell does that mean?
And I was like --

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:16.115
And "You cook a lot!
You cook a lot on daytime."

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.384
-You cook a lot on daytime!
That's just part of the gig.

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:20.452
-I did learn some tips, though,
so there you go.

00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:23.055
-And you guys are starting
the season in New York?

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.823
-We are.
We have two premiere weeks.

00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:26.859
We have --
The first one is in New York,

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:29.528
and we're shooting it right now,
and then we have one in L.A.

00:05:29.528 --> 00:05:31.096
So, we're kind of like --

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:33.065
Everybody wants their
lives back, right?

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:35.168
And we're slowly starting
to get there, right?

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:37.503
-Yeah.
-And -- or trying desperately.

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:40.906
And so we came to New York,
and we're celebrating two cities

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:42.375
that people know of them,
but we're kind of representing

00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:44.776
all the cities, though.
Like, just trying to get back

00:05:44.776 --> 00:05:46.813
to whatever normal
shall be, but...

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:47.980
-Yeah.
-But just like --

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.448
'Cause our whole show
is about connection.

00:05:49.448 --> 00:05:51.383
This is irony.
And then, right in the middle

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:53.118
of our first season
of connecting, like,

00:05:53.118 --> 00:05:54.886
COVID happened and, like,
everyone was isolated.

00:05:54.886 --> 00:05:56.756
-Yeah.
And you go, "That's --"

00:05:56.756 --> 00:05:57.990
Exactly, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.693
So, that was kind of crushing.
So, yeah.

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:03.328
-But I'm happy that you
continued to do your show,

00:06:03.328 --> 00:06:05.464
because we all
needed something to watch.

00:06:05.464 --> 00:06:07.667
It's like, that's the best time
to step up.

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.769
-This is a question for you.
Okay. So, people --

00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:11.204
Literally, somebody
just told me this.

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:13.338
They're like, "We're so happy
you kept doing it during the --"

00:06:13.338 --> 00:06:15.474
Was that hard for you to, like,
keep going, like,

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:18.277
even if it was from --
'Cause we are humans, too,

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.279
and we have our own stuff
in our own backpacks, like,

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:22.147
going through stuff,
and you're having to smile

00:06:22.147 --> 00:06:23.383
and make jokes and like --

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:24.650
and that was kind of hard
for a minute.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:25.952
-It was tough, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:27.352
Yeah, 'cause you don't --
'Cause you're, like,

00:06:27.352 --> 00:06:28.854
trying to be like a cheerleader,
to make people feel better.

00:06:28.854 --> 00:06:30.656
-It was my wife --
My wife, I'll give her credit.

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:31.924
-Really?
-It was Nancy, yeah.

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.659
'Cause I was like --
She was like, "What's the plan?"

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:35.461
I go, "No one knows
what's going on.

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:37.696
We have no plan.
We're just gonna wait it out."

00:06:37.696 --> 00:06:40.600
And she goes,
"Oh, no. You go out to Lowe's,

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.969
or wherever it is -- Target."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:44.903
-You build a studio.
-"You build a studio."

00:06:44.903 --> 00:06:47.239
-You lazy man.
-Yeah, and she goes,

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:49.542
"And you hold your phone up,
and you do a show.

00:06:49.542 --> 00:06:51.110
Broadcast from --
'Cause people need you now."

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:54.247
-Okay. Well, I was in Montana
in like 6 feet of snow.

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:56.382
Like, and everybody was like,
"Let's get into this show."

00:06:56.382 --> 00:06:58.751
And I was like, "Sure."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.853
-I'm so happy you did, though.
-I'm glad I did,

00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:03.122
'cause when you do focus
on helping others,

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:04.723
I think it helps you
through your own stuff.

00:07:04.723 --> 00:07:06.158
-I totally agree.
-So, yeah.

00:07:06.158 --> 00:07:08.093
-I want to talk to you
about "The Voice."

00:07:08.093 --> 00:07:09.561
-I got way off
of track somewhere.

00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:10.930
-No, I love it.
Please.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.665
-The whole pre-interview
is screwed.

00:07:12.665 --> 00:07:14.633
-Yeah, this is --
Yeah, we don't need any of this.

00:07:14.633 --> 00:07:15.801
We didn't stick to any of this.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:15.801 --> 00:07:16.869
-We don't know any of it.
-It doesn't matter.

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:18.204
It doesn't matter.
We're having fun.

